            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Background</h2>
<p>During the cold launch phase of a new product on TikTok Shop, the biggest risk isn't the product itself—it's unclear creative direction leading to budget waste. Most teams' problem: they start advertising first and create assets as they go, resulting in insufficient data accumulation for the algorithm to optimize.</p>
<p>This article solves that problem—with an SOP approach to build a "ready-to-use creative pool" before launching ads, giving you sufficient ammunition for A/B testing once the account goes live.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Define Creative Direction (Day 1)</h2>
<h3>1.1 List Product Selling Points</h3>
<p>Before filming anything, answer this question: <strong>Why would users buy this product on TikTok?</strong></p>
<p>List 3-5 core selling points across these dimensions:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Functional benefits</strong>: What specific problem does it solve?</li>
<li><strong>Emotional value</strong>: The emotional experience from the use case</li>
<li><strong>Price anchor</strong>: Advantages compared to similar products</li>
<li><strong>Differentiating features</strong>: Appearance, materials, patents, certifications</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Practical tip</strong>: Use an Excel sheet with selling points on the left and corresponding creative formats on the right. One utility app team used this method to narrow 12 selling points to 4 high-frequency directions, significantly improving production efficiency.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>1.2 Analyze Competitor Creatives</h3>
<p>Enter product keywords in TikTok Creative Center or similar tools to export top-performing videos from the past 30 days. Focus on:</p>
<ul>
<li>Video length of viral content (first 3 seconds)</li>
<li>Common presentation styles (unboxing vs.场景 vs. comparison)</li>
<li>User concerns in comments (use as creative script reference)</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Plan Creative Types (Day 2)</h2>
<p>A creative pool doesn't need every piece to be a "blockbuster"—but it needs diverse formats to validate user preferences. Here are 5 recommended basic types:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Creative Type</th>
<th>Duration</th>
<th>Best For</th>
<th>Production Cost</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Product Unboxing</strong></td>
<td>15-30s</td>
<td>Building trust, showing real appearance</td>
<td>Low</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Use Case Scenario</strong></td>
<td>15-20s</td>
<td>Creating empathy, driving impulse buys</td>
<td>Medium</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Before/After</strong></td>
<td>20-30s</td>
<td>Highlighting effects (beauty/tools)</td>
<td>Medium</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>UGC-style Talking</strong></td>
<td>10-20s</td>
<td>Enhancing authenticity, casual presenter</td>
<td>Low</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Product Close-up + Text</strong></td>
<td>10-15s</td>
<td>Information-dense, functional products</td>
<td>Low</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Minimum Pool Configuration</strong>: At least 2 pieces per type, totaling <strong>10 pieces minimum</strong>, covering 3+ core selling points.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Production Standards (Day 3-4)</h2>
<h3>3.1 Visual Requirements</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Vertical 9:16 format</strong>, resolution 1080×1920</li>
<li><strong>Adequate lighting</strong>, avoid backlight or dark scenes</li>
<li>Product occupies 60%+ of the frame, minimize irrelevant elements</li>
<li>Text information centered in the middle 2/3 of the frame (avoid TikTok's bottom UI overlap)</li>
</ul>
<h3>3.2 The 3-Second Hook Rule</h3>
<p>Users swipe quickly. The first 3 seconds must include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hook question</strong>: "Do you know the correct way to use XXX?"</li>
<li><strong>Visual impact</strong>: Product close-up, comparison contrast</li>
<li><strong>Numbers/data</strong>: "Works in 3 days" "Save 80% time"</li>
</ul>
<h3>3.3 Script Template (Ready to Use)</h3>
<pre><code>[0-3s] Hook: Create suspense or pose a question
[3-8s] Pain point: Describe real user frustrations
[8-20s] Solution: Show how the product solves it
[20-25s] Trust: Real person or credentials
[25-30s] CTA: Guide to click buy/comment
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Testing &amp; Data Collection (Day 5-6)</h2>
<h3>4.1 Test Setup</h3>
<ul>
<li>Create <strong>one ad group per creative</strong> to avoid internal competition</li>
<li>Budget: <strong>Daily budget per ad group = Expected CPA × 20</strong> to ensure sufficient conversion data</li>
<li>Duration: <strong>Minimum 48 hours</strong> to avoid misjudging from first-day fluctuations</li>
</ul>
<h3>4.2 Key Metrics</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Pass Threshold</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>3s View Rate</td>
<td>&gt;45%</td>
<td>Is the opening engaging?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Completion Rate</td>
<td>&gt;25%</td>
<td>Does content retain users?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CTR</td>
<td>&gt;1.5%</td>
<td>Are clicks above industry average?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CVR</td>
<td>&gt;3%</td>
<td>Is landing page converting?</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Focus</strong>: During cold launch, prioritize <strong>3s View Rate</strong> and <strong>CVR</strong>—these determine if a creative deserves continued investment.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>4.3 Creative Grading</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Grade A</strong>: CVR &gt; 5% → Enter main campaign rotation</li>
<li><strong>Grade B</strong>: CVR 3-5% → Continue observing or minor adjustments</li>
<li><strong>Grade C</strong>: CVR &lt; 3% → Pause or re-edit</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Iteration &amp; Expansion (Day 7+)</h2>
<h3>5.1 Iteration Logic</h3>
<p>After collecting test data, adjust in this priority order:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Expand variants of Grade A creatives</strong>: Change scenery, music, text styles</li>
<li><strong>Fix weaknesses in Grade B creatives</strong>: If CTR is low, replace the opening; if CVR is low, optimize landing page</li>
<li><strong>Retire Grade C creatives</strong>: Save production resources</li>
</ol>
<h3>5.2 Pool Maintenance</h3>
<ul>
<li>Add <strong>3-5 new creatives weekly</strong> to keep the pool fresh</li>
<li>Top-performing creatives have a <strong>2-3 week lifecycle</strong>—prepare replacements in advance</li>
<li>Build a creative tagging system (by product, function, style) for quick retrieval</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q: Limited budget—10 creatives is too many?</strong></p>
<p>A: Prioritize 2 pieces each for <strong>Unboxing + Use Case + Talking Head</strong>—just 6 total. Test the most likely winning direction first.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Poor test data—is it a creative or product issue?</strong></p>
<p>A: Check CTR first—if click rate is low, it's likely a creative problem. If clicks are good but CVR is low, optimize the landing page or re-evaluate product-market fit.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Does UGC content need real users on camera?</strong></p>
<p>A: During cold launch, employees or foreign actors work fine—you don't need authentic buyers yet. The key is authentic-feeling content style, not necessarily casual presenters.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Closing</h2>
<p>A creative pool isn't built once and forgotten—it's a continuous cycle of addition, testing, and retirement. The core goal during cold launch is validating direction at minimum cost. Once you find creatives that work, scale immediately. We hope this SOP helps your team shift from a reactive "figure it out while running ads" mindset to a proactive "confident execution" approach.</p>
